{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SBNMBNXxHt33Kz1zjYrgpF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CvkPMyF9chaBmeFZAkEjK5","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the paper below according to top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Dual-Constrained Adaptive Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining\n\nThe paper proposes \"dual-constrained adaptive b*\" for selecting the reweighting exponent b in contamination-aware LM pretraining. Instead of grid-searching b in the weight formula w=(1-q)^b, the method derives b per micro-batch from two constraints:\n1. α_eff(b) ≥ τ: effective real-data fraction lower bound (quality floor)\n2. ESS(b) ≥ n_min: Kish effective sample size ceiling (stability ceiling)\n\nThe solution b* = inf{b≥0 : α_eff(b)≥τ} subject to ESS(b)≥n_min embodies a \"minimum intervention principle.\"\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n\n**Table 1 (Main result):** Adaptive b* vs grid-best (b=0.5) on Qwen3-0.6B, ρ=0.4:\n- Uniform: 17.690/17.690/17.700 (seeds 42/123/456)\n- Grid-best: 17.651/17.638/17.650\n- Adaptive: 17.650/17.643/17.660\n- Recovery ratio: 102.6%/90.4%/80.0% (aggregate 88.6%)\n\n**Table 2 (Extended grid):** b∈{0.1,0.2,0.3,0.5} × 3 seeds. Broad plateau [0.3,0.5], paired t-test b=0.3 vs b=0.5 p=0.42.\n\n**Table 3 (ESS-only ablation):** Dual-constraint PPL=17.650 vs ESS-only PPL=17.685 (near uniform 17.690). In α_eff binding batches, b* goes from 1.67 (dual) to 4.18 (ESS-only).\n\n**Table 4 (τ sweep):** τ∈{0.5,0.6,0.7,0.8,0.9,0.95}, PPL range=0.023, std=0.008. All beat uniform and ESS-only.\n\n**Table 5 (n_min sensitivity):** n_min=1→17.700 (over-aggressive), n_min=2→17.650 (optimal), n_min=4→17.690 (=uniform), n_min=5→17.690 (degenerate). Note: n_min=3 is skipped.\n\n**Table 6 (Batch size ablation):** |B|=4→17.650, |B|=8→17.643, |B|=16→17.638. α_eff binding increases from 20.2% to 35.2%.\n\n**Table 7 (Contamination scaling):** ρ=0.4 adaptive Δ=0.040, ρ=0.6 adaptive Δ=0.100, 2.5× amplification. Grid b=0.5 at ρ=0.6 only gets Δ=0.050.\n\n**Table 8 (Cross-model):** Qwen3-0.6B Δ=0.040, Llama-3.2-3B Δ=0.160, 4× amplification. b* median nearly identical (0.87 vs 0.88).\n\n**Table 9 (Baselines):** Random reweight≈uniform (17.695), detect-and-remove=17.668 (55% of adaptive), Golden Ratio oracle=17.665.\n\n**Table 10 (Detector quality):** TF-IDF AUC=0.939→PPL 17.650, ModernBERT AUC=0.986→PPL 17.638. TF-IDF captures 77%.\n\n**Table 11 (Downstream, 3B):** MMLU +0.7%, HellaSwag +1.1%, Self-BLEU -0.04.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiments)\nThe full experiment plan included 24 imagined experiments. The paper covers most but notable gaps:\n- n_min=3 is skipped in Table 5 (context shows it should be \"fragile\" at 17.690)\n- Cross-generator results are mentioned in text (§5.11) but no table\n- Domain-skewed results mentioned in text (§5.12) but no table\n\n## Your Review Tasks\n\nEvaluate the following with specific evidence:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the dual-constraint formulation well-motivated? Any gaps in the mathematical framework? Is the bisection implementation adequate? Are there edge cases not addressed?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**: \n   - Are the controlled contamination experiments convincing? \n   - Is the evaluation on 200M tokens sufficient?\n   - Are the baselines fair and comprehensive?\n   - Is statistical reporting adequate (seeds, significance tests)?\n   - Are there missing experiments that would strengthen the claims?\n\n3. **Effect sizes and practical significance**: The PPL improvements are small in absolute terms (0.04 at 0.6B). Is the 4× amplification claim at 3B convincing? Are downstream improvements meaningful?\n\n4. **Reproducibility concerns**: Is Algorithm 1 sufficient for reproduction? Are hyperparameter choices well-justified?\n\n5. **Specific weaknesses** you identify in methods or experiments.\n\n6. **Your preliminary score** (1-6) and confidence (1-5).\n\nFormat your response as a structured review with sections for Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, and Score."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":43610,"cache_read_input_tokens":22934,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":43610},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd49d1e7-888b-4772-96eb-3b1102b8f535","uuid":"394c4af1-2d3a-4bbb-bd9a-991bc4df6a25"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a6fc820c98a05d8ff","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CvkPMyF9chaBmeFZAkEjK5","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the paper below according to top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Dual-Constrained Adaptive Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining\n\nThe paper proposes \"dual-constrained adaptive b*\" for selecting the reweighting exponent b in contamination-aware LM pretraining. Instead of grid-searching b in the weight formula w=(1-q)^b, the method derives b per micro-batch from two constraints:\n1. α_eff(b) ≥ τ: effective real-data fraction lower bound (quality floor)\n2. ESS(b) ≥ n_min: Kish effective sample size ceiling (stability ceiling)\n\nThe solution b* = inf{b≥0 : α_eff(b)≥τ} subject to ESS(b)≥n_min embodies a \"minimum intervention principle.\"\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n\n**Table 1 (Main result):** Adaptive b* vs grid-best (b=0.5) on Qwen3-0.6B, ρ=0.4:\n- Uniform: 17.690/17.690/17.700 (seeds 42/123/456)\n- Grid-best: 17.651/17.638/17.650\n- Adaptive: 17.650/17.643/17.660\n- Recovery ratio: 102.6%/90.4%/80.0% (aggregate 88.6%)\n\n**Table 2 (Extended grid):** b∈{0.1,0.2,0.3,0.5} × 3 seeds. Broad plateau [0.3,0.5], paired t-test b=0.3 vs b=0.5 p=0.42.\n\n**Table 3 (ESS-only ablation):** Dual-constraint PPL=17.650 vs ESS-only PPL=17.685 (near uniform 17.690). In α_eff binding batches, b* goes from 1.67 (dual) to 4.18 (ESS-only).\n\n**Table 4 (τ sweep):** τ∈{0.5,0.6,0.7,0.8,0.9,0.95}, PPL range=0.023, std=0.008. All beat uniform and ESS-only.\n\n**Table 5 (n_min sensitivity):** n_min=1→17.700 (over-aggressive), n_min=2→17.650 (optimal), n_min=4→17.690 (=uniform), n_min=5→17.690 (degenerate). Note: n_min=3 is skipped.\n\n**Table 6 (Batch size ablation):** |B|=4→17.650, |B|=8→17.643, |B|=16→17.638. α_eff binding increases from 20.2% to 35.2%.\n\n**Table 7 (Contamination scaling):** ρ=0.4 adaptive Δ=0.040, ρ=0.6 adaptive Δ=0.100, 2.5× amplification. Grid b=0.5 at ρ=0.6 only gets Δ=0.050.\n\n**Table 8 (Cross-model):** Qwen3-0.6B Δ=0.040, Llama-3.2-3B Δ=0.160, 4× amplification. b* median nearly identical (0.87 vs 0.88).\n\n**Table 9 (Baselines):** Random reweight≈uniform (17.695), detect-and-remove=17.668 (55% of adaptive), Golden Ratio oracle=17.665.\n\n**Table 10 (Detector quality):** TF-IDF AUC=0.939→PPL 17.650, ModernBERT AUC=0.986→PPL 17.638. TF-IDF captures 77%.\n\n**Table 11 (Downstream, 3B):** MMLU +0.7%, HellaSwag +1.1%, Self-BLEU -0.04.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiments)\nThe full experiment plan included 24 imagined experiments. The paper covers most but notable gaps:\n- n_min=3 is skipped in Table 5 (context shows it should be \"fragile\" at 17.690)\n- Cross-generator results are mentioned in text (§5.11) but no table\n- Domain-skewed results mentioned in text (§5.12) but no table\n\n## Your Review Tasks\n\nEvaluate the following with specific evidence:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the dual-constraint formulation well-motivated? Any gaps in the mathematical framework? Is the bisection implementation adequate? Are there edge cases not addressed?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**: \n   - Are the controlled contamination experiments convincing? \n   - Is the evaluation on 200M tokens sufficient?\n   - Are the baselines fair and comprehensive?\n   - Is statistical reporting adequate (seeds, significance tests)?\n   - Are there missing experiments that would strengthen the claims?\n\n3. **Effect sizes and practical significance**: The PPL improvements are small in absolute terms (0.04 at 0.6B). Is the 4× amplification claim at 3B convincing? Are downstream improvements meaningful?\n\n4. **Reproducibility concerns**: Is Algorithm 1 sufficient for reproduction? Are hyperparameter choices well-justified?\n\n5. **Specific weaknesses** you identify in methods or experiments.\n\n6. **Your preliminary score** (1-6) and confidence (1-5).\n\nFormat your response as a structured review with sections for Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, and Score.","uuid":"9b74ff33-7cbd-4078-abf1-9129fb8dec4d","session_id":"cd49d1e7-888b-4772-96eb-3b1102b8f535"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Review the paper below according to top ML venue standards (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Dual-Constrained Adaptive Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining\n\nThe paper proposes \"dual-constrained adaptive b*\" for selecting the reweighting exponent b in contamination-aware LM pretraining. Instead of grid-searching b in the weight formula w=(1-q)^b, the method derives b per micro-batch from two constraints:\n1. α_eff(b) ≥ τ: effective real-data fraction lower bound (quality floor)\n2. ESS(b) ≥ n_min: Kish effective sample size ceiling (stability ceiling)\n\nThe solution b* = inf{b≥0 : α_eff(b)≥τ} subject to ESS(b)≥n_min embodies a \"minimum intervention principle.\"\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n\n**Table 1 (Main result):** Adaptive b* vs grid-best (b=0.5) on Qwen3-0.6B, ρ=0.4:\n- Uniform: 17.690/17.690/17.700 (seeds 42/123/456)\n- Grid-best: 17.651/17.638/17.650\n- Adaptive: 17.650/17.643/17.660\n- Recovery ratio: 102.6%/90.4%/80.0% (aggregate 88.6%)\n\n**Table 2 (Extended grid):** b∈{0.1,0.2,0.3,0.5} × 3 seeds. Broad plateau [0.3,0.5], paired t-test b=0.3 vs b=0.5 p=0.42.\n\n**Table 3 (ESS-only ablation):** Dual-constraint PPL=17.650 vs ESS-only PPL=17.685 (near uniform 17.690). In α_eff binding batches, b* goes from 1.67 (dual) to 4.18 (ESS-only).\n\n**Table 4 (τ sweep):** τ∈{0.5,0.6,0.7,0.8,0.9,0.95}, PPL range=0.023, std=0.008. All beat uniform and ESS-only.\n\n**Table 5 (n_min sensitivity):** n_min=1→17.700 (over-aggressive), n_min=2→17.650 (optimal), n_min=4→17.690 (=uniform), n_min=5→17.690 (degenerate). Note: n_min=3 is skipped.\n\n**Table 6 (Batch size ablation):** |B|=4→17.650, |B|=8→17.643, |B|=16→17.638. α_eff binding increases from 20.2% to 35.2%.\n\n**Table 7 (Contamination scaling):** ρ=0.4 adaptive Δ=0.040, ρ=0.6 adaptive Δ=0.100, 2.5× amplification. Grid b=0.5 at ρ=0.6 only gets Δ=0.050.\n\n**Table 8 (Cross-model):** Qwen3-0.6B Δ=0.040, Llama-3.2-3B Δ=0.160, 4× amplification. b* median nearly identical (0.87 vs 0.88).\n\n**Table 9 (Baselines):** Random reweight≈uniform (17.695), detect-and-remove=17.668 (55% of adaptive), Golden Ratio oracle=17.665.\n\n**Table 10 (Detector quality):** TF-IDF AUC=0.939→PPL 17.650, ModernBERT AUC=0.986→PPL 17.638. TF-IDF captures 77%.\n\n**Table 11 (Downstream, 3B):** MMLU +0.7%, HellaSwag +1.1%, Self-BLEU -0.04.\n\n## Review Context (Imagined Experiments)\nThe full experiment plan included 24 imagined experiments. The paper covers most but notable gaps:\n- n_min=3 is skipped in Table 5 (context shows it should be \"fragile\" at 17.690)\n- Cross-generator results are mentioned in text (§5.11) but no table\n- Domain-skewed results mentioned in text (§5.12) but no table\n\n## Your Review Tasks\n\nEvaluate the following with specific evidence:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the dual-constraint formulation well-motivated? Any gaps in the mathematical framework? Is the bisection implementation adequate? Are there edge cases not addressed?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**: \n   - Are the controlled contamination experiments convincing? \n   - Is the evaluation on 200M tokens sufficient?\n   - Are the baselines fair and comprehensive?\n   - Is statistical reporting adequate (seeds, significance tests)?\n   - Are there missing experiments that would strengthen the claims?\n\n3. **Effect sizes and practical significance**: The PPL improvements are small in absolute terms (0.04 at 0.6B). Is the 4× amplification claim at 3B convincing? Are downstream improvements meaningful?\n\n4. **Reproducibility concerns**: Is Algorithm 1 sufficient for reproduction? Are hyperparameter choices well-justified?\n\n5. **Specific weaknesses** you identify in methods or experiments.\n\n6. **Your preliminary score** (1-6) and confidence (1-5).\n\nFormat your response as a structured review with sections for Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, and Score."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CvkPMyF9chaBmeFZAkEjK5","session_id":"cd49d1e7-888b-4772-96eb-3b1102b8f535","uuid":"09183e65-ffff-46f5-ad8f-1e304a989453","timestamp":"2026-05-15T11:00:57.936Z"}
