{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DXyRagwFjsQ6My8f5yFa7H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F6C6v1rJSqCNw4BScj2SEu","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Dream Review Report: Dual-Constrained Adaptive Reweighting for Contamination-Robust Language Model Pretraining\n\n**dream_id:** 20260515T102146 | **review_mode:** full | **reviewers:** R1 (Methods+Experiments), R2 (Novelty+Related Work)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThe paper proposes *dual-constrained adaptive b**, a method for selecting the reweighting exponent b in the contamination-aware pretraining formula w=(1−q)^b. Instead of Drayson et al.'s per-model grid search over b, the method derives b per micro-batch from two constraints: (1) an effective real-data fraction lower bound (α_eff(b) ≥ τ) ensuring sufficient decontamination, and (2) a Kish effective sample size ceiling (ESS(b) ≥ n_min) preventing over-downweighting. The solution b* = inf{b ≥ 0 : α_eff(b) ≥ τ} s.t. ESS(b) ≥ n_min embodies a \"minimum intervention principle.\" Experiments on Qwen3-0.6B and Llama-3.2-3B (200M tokens, 40–60% contamination) show adaptive b* matches grid-best within seed variance, with effect amplification at higher contamination (2.5×) and larger model scale (4×).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nRelative to the full experiment design (24 imagined experiments), the paper covers approximately 20–21:\n\n| Experiment Category | Covered? | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Grid-b extended (b<0.5, 3 seeds + eval) | ✓ | Table 2, 4 data points |\n| ESS-only ablation | ✓ | Table 3, seed42 only |\n| τ sweep (6 values) | ✓ | Table 4 |\n| Golden Ratio oracle | ✓ | §5.5 |\n| n_min sensitivity | Partial | Table 5 — **n_min=3 omitted** |\n| Batch size ablation (8, 16) | ✓ | Table 6 |\n| ρ=0.6 scaling (datagen + uniform + adaptive + grid) | ✓ | Table 7 |\n| Llama-3.2-3B (setup + uniform + adaptive) | ✓ | Table 8 |\n| Baselines (detect-remove, random) | ✓ | Table 9 |\n| Detector quality (ModernBERT) | ✓ | Table 10 |\n| Cross-generator | ✓ | §5.11 (text only, no table) |\n| Domain-skewed | ✓ | §5.12 (text only, no table) |\n| Downstream eval (MMLU, HellaSwag, Self-BLEU) | ✓ | Table 11 |\n\n**Key gaps:** n_min=3 omitted from Table 5; cross-generator and domain-skewed lack dedicated tables; amplification claims (3B, ρ=0.6) are single-seed.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Reviewer R1 — Methods and Experiments\n\n### Score: 3 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Strengths\n- **S1:** The minimum intervention formulation is mathematically clean. α_eff(b) monotonicity (via Chebyshev's order inequality) guarantees well-defined bisection.\n- **S2:** The constraint binding analysis is informative: 40% of reweighted batches are α_eff-bound (b*=1.67 vs ESS-only b*=4.15, a 2.5× reduction in intervention).\n- **S3:** Cross-seed stability of b* distribution is high (CV < 2.5%), confirming b* is a data property.\n- **S4:** Experimental controls are careful: identical hyperparameters, consistent holdout evaluation, 3 seeds for primary experiments.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **W1 (Major): Small effect sizes.** Absolute improvement is 0.040 PPL (0.23% relative) at 0.6B. The adaptive-vs-grid-best gap is ~0.005 PPL — within measurement precision. The 4× amplification at 3B (Δ=0.160) and 2.5× at ρ=0.6 (Δ=0.100) are single-seed claims without multi-seed confirmation.\n- **W2 (Major): n_min knife-edge at batch_size=4.** Table 5 shows only n_min=2 works (n_min=1→17.700, n_min=4→17.690=uniform). n_min=3 is omitted — this is suspicious given it would show the transition region. The method operates at its constraint boundary in 75%+ of batches.\n- **W3 (Major): τ insensitivity paradox.** The τ sweep (Table 4, PPL range=0.023) is presented as a strength, but it undermines the α_eff constraint's importance: if PPL barely changes across τ∈[0.5,0.95], why not just use b=0.5 with no adaptive machinery?\n- **W4 (Moderate): \"Zero tuning cost\" is overstated.** The method replaces one hyperparameter (b) with two (τ, n_min). If b has a broad plateau at [0.3,0.5] (Table 2), a practitioner could set b=0.5 universally with comparable results.\n- **W5 (Moderate): ESS-only ablation is single-seed.** Table 3's critical result (17.685 vs 17.650) needs multi-seed validation. The Δ=0.035 exceeds estimated seed variance (σ≈0.009), but 3-seed confirmation would be much stronger.\n- **W6 (Minor): Algorithm 1 underspecification.** Grid resolution (1000 points), b_max=20.0, and fallback behavior when constraints conflict are not fully described.\n\n### Questions\n1. In only 20.2% of all micro-batches is α_eff binding. How confident are you that this minority of batches drives the improvement rather than ESS-constrained batches?\n2. How should n_min scale with batch size? Is n_min=2 always appropriate?\n3. The adaptive b* median (0.88) is ~2× the grid-best (b=0.5). Why does the method still recover 91% of grid-best improvement despite systematically overshooting?\n4. If τ is this insensitive, could you remove α_eff and use ESS-only with tuned n_min?\n\n---\n\n## 4. Reviewer R2 — Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Score: 4 (Weak Accept) | Confidence: 4/5\n\n### Strengths\n- **S1:** The minimum intervention principle (inf vs max) is a genuine conceptual contribution. The distinction between \"minimum necessary cleaning\" and \"maximum tolerable cleaning\" is non-obvious and empirically validated by the ESS-only comparison.\n- **S2:** Transparent experimental methodology with honest limitations disclosure.\n- **S3:** TF-IDF detector sufficiency (77% of ModernBERT value at ~100× lower cost) is a practically valuable finding.\n- **S4:** Clean delineation from Drayson et al. — the paper does not overclaim the weighting formula as novel.\n- **S5:** The α_eff monotonicity proof is correct and ensures bisection well-definedness.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **W1 (Major): Narrow novelty.** Both α_eff (weighted mean) and ESS (Kish's) are well-established. Combining them to select a single scalar in an existing formula is sensible engineering but not a methodological advance. The paper overstates generality by calling this a \"dual-constrained framework.\"\n- **W2 (Major): \"Dataset-agnostic defaults\" unsupported.** Evidence comes from one contamination ratio, one model, one batch size, one data composition. n_min has a documented failure mode (n_min=3 at bs=4).\n- **W3 (Major): Conflation of \"adaptive\" with \"automatic.\"** The method still requires choosing τ and n_min; these needed non-trivial tuning during development.\n- **W4 (Moderate): Hard threshold q≤0.5 in α_eff is poorly motivated.** The indicator at 0.5 is arbitrary for imperfect detectors and introduces a discontinuity. The paper should justify this choice.\n- **W5 (Moderate): Important related work missing.**\n  - Vehtari et al. (JMLR 2024) on PSIS and ESS diagnostics — directly relevant, uncited\n  - Propensity score methods / inverse probability weighting (Rosenbaum & Rubin, 1983) — structural parallel to α_eff\n  - Off-policy RL importance weighting — ESS-constrained IS is standard practice\n  - Self-paced / curriculum learning — adaptive per-sample weighting during training\n  - AIOLI (Chen et al., ICLR 2025) — adaptive online data mixing, closest \"no grid search\" comparator\n- **W6 (Minor): Abstract claim \"matches grid-search-optimal within seed variance\" is imprecise.** Aggregate recovery is 88.6–91%. Seeds 123 (90.4%) and 456 (80.0%) do not literally match \"within seed variance\" in the statistical sense.\n\n### Questions\n1. Which α_eff definition is actually implemented — the hard indicator version or the continuous x^{b+1}/x^b version?\n2. If ESS-only matches dual-constraint performance, what remains of the contribution?\n3. Has the method been tested at batch_size ≥ 32 where ESS dynamic range is much larger?\n4. Why does b* median (0.88) work well despite being ~2× grid-best (0.5)?\n5. The convergence theory motivation for τ (Wang et al.) requires known contamination proportion. How does this provide guidance when ρ is unknown?\n\n---\n\n## 5. Roundtable Synthesis\n\n### Areas of Agreement\n1. **Effect sizes are marginal at 0.6B** — both reviewers flag 0.04 PPL (0.23%) as borderline for practical significance.\n2. **n_min knife-edge at bs=4 is a real concern** — only one integer value works; n_min=3 omission is suspicious.\n3. **τ insensitivity is a double-edged sword** — supports robustness but undermines α_eff's claimed importance.\n4. **\"Dataset-agnostic defaults\" is overclaimed** — single-setup evidence is insufficient.\n5. **Minimum intervention principle is the genuine contribution** — both reviewers agree this conceptual framing is non-trivial and empirically validated.\n6. **Experiment coverage is high** — ~20/24 imagined experiments are present, methodology is careful and honest.\n\n### Areas of Disagreement\n- **R1 (3) vs R2 (4):** R1 weighs narrow operating regime and marginal effect sizes more heavily; R2 gives more credit to the conceptual contribution and practical utility.\n- **R2 identifies substantial related work gaps** (PSIS, causal inference, off-policy RL) not raised by R1.\n- **R2 flags the α_eff definition inconsistency** (hard indicator vs continuous) as a clarity issue.\n\n### Converged Assessment\n\nThe paper addresses a timely and important problem (synthetic contamination of pretraining data). The dual-constraint formulation is mathematically clean, the minimum intervention principle is a legitimate conceptual contribution, and the experimental coverage is comprehensive with honest reporting of limitations. The TF-IDF sufficiency finding adds practical value.\n\nHowever, the paper has compounding weaknesses that place it at the borderline:\n- Effect sizes are small enough to question practical significance at the tested scale\n- The key amplification claims (3B, ρ=0.6) lack multi-seed confirmation\n- The method introduces two hyperparameters to replace one, with n_min having a narrow working range at small batch sizes\n- τ insensitivity paradoxically weakens the case for α_eff\n- Important related work in importance sampling diagnostics is missing\n- The \"dataset-agnostic\" framing overreaches the evidence\n\n---\n\n## 6. Overall Score\n\n### Consensus: 4 — Weak Accept (borderline)\n\n| Criterion | Score | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Novelty | 3.5 | Modest — applying known IS diagnostics to a specific b-selection problem |\n| Soundness | 4.5 | Formulation is correct; experiments are careful |\n| Significance | 3.0 | Small effect sizes at tested scale; amplification claims under-supported |\n| Clarity | 4.0 | Well-written with clean notation; some claim inflation in abstract |\n| Experiment coverage | 4.5 | Comprehensive ablations; most imagined experiments covered |\n| Reproducibility | 4.0 | Algorithm 1 mostly sufficient; some implementation details missing |\n\n**Overall: 4 (Weak Accept) — borderline, conditional on revisions**\n\n### Required Revisions for Acceptance\n1. **Add n_min=3 to Table 5** — the omission undermines credibility\n2. **Multi-seed confirmation for 3B and ρ=0.6 experiments** — single-seed amplification claims are insufficient\n3. **Cite PSIS (Vehtari et al.) and connect to off-policy IS literature** — the ESS diagnostic is not novel and should be properly attributed\n4. **Tone down \"dataset-agnostic defaults\"** to \"insensitive within the tested configuration\"\n5. **Clarify the α_eff definition** — hard indicator vs continuous formulation discrepancy\n6. **Add paired significance test for adaptive vs grid-best** across 3 seeds\n7. **Add confidence intervals for downstream metrics** (Table 11)\n8. **Tabulate cross-generator and domain-skewed results** instead of text-only mention\n\n### Recommended Revisions\n- Add batch_size=32/64 experiments to demonstrate n_min=2 stability at practical scales\n- Provide wall-clock timing measurements for the bisection overhead\n- Discuss the gradient variance implications of minimum vs maximum intervention formally\n- Correct abstract claim from \"within seed variance\" to \"approaching grid-search-optimal\"\n","project":"provenance_weight_training","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260515T102146","overall_score":"4 - Weak Accept (borderline)","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":769,"cache_read_input_tokens":80081,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":769},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cd49d1e7-888b-4772-96eb-3b1102b8f535","uuid":"5d46b074-8e4d-4b30-9731-1fff30975b5f"}
